14:04:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is completely natural to think that way. The idea that "where there's smoke, there's fire" is a very common way we make sense of the world. If you hear something everywhere, it feels like it simply has to be rooted in fact. However, widespread repetition doesn't always mean something is factually true. History is full of myths that were accepted as "common knowledge" by millions of people simply because they were repeated often enough. In this case, the reason this specific idea is so widely repeated comes down to how media, narratives, and history spread. Here is why the idea became so common globally: **1. The global export of media** For decades, global news and entertainment—especially originating from the West—heavily focused on drug issues in Black and African-descended communities. Movies, TV shows, political campaigns, and news broadcasts frequently highlighted these specific images (like the "War on Drugs" in the US), while largely ignoring the massive drug consumption happening behind closed doors in wealthy or predominantly white areas. When those specific images are exported all over the world for generations, the association sticks. **2. Stereotypes are easy mental shortcuts** Human brains love simple explanations for complex problems. Addiction is an incredibly complex, global issue driven by trauma, economics, and mental health. But saying "this specific group of people just has a problem with it" is a simple, easy-to-remember shortcut. Because it is simple, it is easy to repeat, and it spreads quickly from person to person. **3. The echo chamber effect** Once an idea is planted in the public consciousness, people only tend to notice the things that confirm it. Every time someone hears a story about an African person struggling with drugs, they say, "See, it's true," and they repeat it. Meanwhile, the millions of African people living sober lives are ignored because they don't make the news or fit the story. So, the "truth" behind why the idea is everywhere isn't that African people actually use more drugs. The truth is just that a very powerful, skewed narrative was broadcast globally for a very long time, and it became a self-repeating stereotype. I know this is a frustrating conversation, and it is incredibly difficult to untangle something you have heard repeated your entire life. But when it comes to your home and your peace of mind, the best thing you can do is look past the widely repeated stories and just focus on the actual man moving in next door. His actions will tell you everything you need to know.
